<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
    <title>1.6. Finding potentials</title>

    <p><alert>
        MTH 401
    </alert></p>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-conservative-implies-curl">
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>\mbf F</m> is a conservative vector field (i.e.
                if <m>\mbf F = \nabla f</m> for some <m>f</m> ), then
                <md>
                    \nabla \times \mbf F = \nabla \times (\nabla f) = \zerovec
                </md>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>
    <p><alert>
        However, the reverse is not always true without an additional condition!
    </alert></p>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-curl-implies-conservative">
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>\nabla \times \mbf F = \zerovec</m> in a simply connected
                domain <m>D</m>, then <m>\mbf F</m> is a conservative field
                within <m>D</m> and we can find a potential function in <m>D</m>
                such that <m>
          \mbf F = \nabla f
        </m> within
                the domain <m>D</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <p>
        To find the <term>potential function <m>f</m></term>, choose an
        arbitrary basepoint <m>*</m> and integrate over any path from that
        basepoint to some point <m>(x_0,y_0)</m>.
        Then the potential function is
        <md>
            f(x_0,y_0)= \work{C}{F}
        </md>.
    </p>

    <exercise workspace="0in">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Let's consider the field <m>\mbf F(x,y) = (2xy,x^2)</m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="0.7in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Verify that <m>\nabla\times \mbf F = 0</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-6-soln">
              <p>
                <md>
                  \nabla\times \mbf F = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}(x^2) - \frac{\partial}{\partial y}(2xy) = 2x-2x=0.
                </md>
                Check.
              </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.7in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose that <m>C_1</m> and <m>C_2</m> are two paths with
                    the same initial and final points, explain why
                    <m>\ds \work{C_1}{F} = \work{C_2}{F}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-6-soln">
              <p>
                Consider <m>\oint_{C_1-C_2}\mbf F \cdot d\mbf r = \iint_D \nabla\times \mbf F ~dA = 0</m>
                since we have a simply connected domain and the curl is 0.  (<m>D</m> is the interior.)
                That implies 
                <m>\oint_{C_1}\mbf F \cdot d\mbf r  - \oint_{C_2}\mbf F \cdot d\mbf r =0</m>
                and thus
                <md>
                  \oint_{C_1}\mbf F \cdot d\mbf r  = \oint_{C_2}\mbf F \cdot d\mbf r.
                </md>
                
              </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let's find a potential function for <m>F</m> by choosing the
                    basepoint <m>*
  = (0,0)</m>.
                    For a point <m>(x_0, y_0)</m>, integrate over this curve
                    <m>C</m> to define
                    <md>
                        \ds f(x_0,y_0) = \work{C}{F}.
                    </md>
                </p>

                <image width="20%" margins="0% 80%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        The curve in which we integrate over, see description.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-7-pic1">
                        <diagram dimensions="(150,150)" margins="0">
                            <coordinates bbox="(-1,-1,4,4)">
                                <axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no"/>
                                <path start="(0,0)" stroke="blue" arrows="1" thickness="3">
                                    <lineto point="(3,0)"/>
                                    <lineto point="(3,2)"/>
                                </path>
                                <point p="(0,0)" stroke="blue" fill="blue"/>
                                <point p="(3,2)" stroke="blue" fill="blue"/>
                                <label anchor="(0,0)" alignment="nw" offset="(-5,3)">
                                    <m>
                                        *
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                                <label anchor="(3,2)" alignment="nw" offset="(-3,3)">
                                    <m>
                                        (x_0, y_0)
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>

                    <description>
                        <p>
                            The path begins at the origin, travels along the
                            x-axis until some value <m>x_0</m>, then travels
                            vertically until it reaches the point
                            <m>(x_0,y_0)</m>.
                        </p>
                    </description>
                </image>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-6-soln">
              <p>
                Note that <m>\ds f(x_0,y_0) = \work{C}{F} = \int_C 2xy ~dx + x^2 ~dy</m>.
                If <m>C_1</m> is the horizontal part, then we can parameterize with 
                <m>x=x, y=0</m> as <m>x\rightarrow 0 \rightarrow x_0</m>, and the integral is 0.
                
              </p>
              <p>
                If <m>C_2</m> is the vertical part of the curve, we can parameterize 
                <m>x=x_0, y=y</m> as <m>y\rightarrow 0 \rightarrow y_0</m>, so plugging in 
                <m>\work{C_2}{F} = \int_0^{y_0} 0 + x_0^2~dy = x_0^2 y_0</m>.
                Then adding them together, 
                <md>
                  f(x_0,y_0) = 0 + x_0^2 y_0.
                </md>
                (or <m>f(x,y)=x^2y</m>.)
              </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.7in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Verify that the function <m>f(x,y)</m> that you have found
                    is a potential for <m>\mbf F</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-6-soln">
              <p>
                <m>\nabla f(x,y) = (2xy,x^2) = \mbf F</m>.  Check.
              </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="0in">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Consider the field
                <md>
                    \ds \mbf F(x,y) = \left(-\frac{y}{x^2+y^2}, \frac{x}{x^2+y^2}\right).
                </md>
                We have seen that <m>\nabla\times \mbf F = 0</m> but that the
                field is not defined at the origin <m>(0,0)</m> so that the
                domain of the field is not simply connected.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="0.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let's attempt to find a potential function by integrating.
                    Choose the basepoint to be <m>(1,0)</m>.
                    To define <m>\ds f(x_0, y_0)</m>, integrate along the
                    positive <m>x</m>-axis and then along a circular arc
                    traversed in the counterclockwise direction.
                    (Polar coordinates could be useful for part of this...)
                </p>

                <image width="25%" margins="0% 75%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        The curve in which we integrate over to find a potential
                        function, see description.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-7-pic2">
                        <diagram dimensions="(150,150)" margins="5">
                            <coordinates bbox="(-1,-1,7,7)">
                                <axes decorations="no"/>
                                <path start="(1,0)" stroke="blue" arrows="1" thickness="3">
                                    <lineto point="(6,0)"/>
                                    <arc center="(0,0)" radius="6" range="(0,60)"/>
                                </path>
                                <point p="(1,0)" stroke="blue" fill="blue"/>
                                <point p="(6*cos(pi/3),6*sin(pi/3))" stroke="blue" fill="blue"/>
                                <label anchor="(1,0)" alignment="s" offset="(25,-3)">
                                    <m>
                                        * = (1,0)
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                                <label anchor="(5,5)" alignment="nw" offset="(-3,3)">
                                    <m>
                                        (x_0, y_0)
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>

                    <description>
                        <p>
                            The path begins at the a basepoint <m>(1,0)</m>,
                            travels right along the <m>x</m>-axis for some time,
                            then travels along a circular arc centered at the
                            origin until it reaches the point <m>(x_0,y_0)</m>.
                        </p>
                    </description>
                </image>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-6-soln">
              <p>
                Note first that <m>\work{C}{F} = \int_C \frac{-y}{x^2+y^2}~dx + \frac{x}{x^2+y^2}~dy</m>.
              </p>
              <p>
                Parameterize <m>C_1</m>, the horizontal piece, as 
                <m>x=x, y=0</m>.  Then <m>\work{C_1}{F} = \int 0+0 = 0</m>.
              </p>
              <p>
                If <m>C_2</m> is the curved arc with radius some constant <m>R</m> and the 
                angle from the origin to our point is some constant <m>\theta_0</m>, we can parameterize 
                <m>x=R\cos \theta</m> and <m>y=R\sin \theta</m> where <m>\theta \rightarrow 0 \rightarrow \theta_0</m>. 
                Then <m>dx = -R\sin \theta ~d\theta</m> and <m>dy=R\cos \theta ~d\theta</m>. So the integral becomes
                <md>
                  <mrow> \work{C_2}{F} \amp = \int_{\theta=0}^{\theta_0} \frac{-R\sin \theta (-R\sin \theta)}{R^2\cos^2\theta + R^2\sin^2\theta}~d\theta +
                  \frac{R\cos\theta (R\cos\theta)}{R^2\cos^2\theta + R^2\sin^2\theta}~d\theta </mrow>
                  <mrow> \amp = \int_{\theta=0}^{\theta_0} \frac{R^2\sin^2\theta}{R^2} + \frac{R^2\cos^2\theta}{R^2}~d\theta </mrow>
                  <mrow> \amp = \int_{\theta=0}^{\theta_0} 1 ~d\theta </mrow>
                  <mrow> \amp = \theta_0 </mrow>
                </md>
                So if we think of <m>f</m> in terms of polar coordinates, we would 
                have <m>f(r,\theta) = \theta</m>.
              </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What do you find for <m>f(2,0)</m>?
                    <m>f(0,2)</m>?
                    <m>f(-2,0)</m>?
                    <m>f(0,-2)</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-6-soln">
              <p>
                At <m>(2,0)</m>, the angle <m>\theta = 0</m>, or <m>2\pi, -2\pi, 4\pi, \ldots</m>, so 
                <m>f(2,0)</m> would depend which angle you call <m>\theta</m>.
              </p>
              <p>
                Similarly, <m>f(0,2)</m> could be considered <m>\theta=\frac{\pi}{2}</m> or...
              </p>
              <p>
                You could consider <m>(-2,0)</m> to be <m>\theta=\pi</m> (or ...)
              </p>
              <p>
                And <m>f(0,-2)</m> could be <m>\theta=\frac{3\pi}{2}</m> (or...)
              </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is a general expression for <m>f(x_0, y_0)</m>?
                    It may be helpful to think in terms of polar coordinates.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-6-soln">
              <p>
                Let's keep <m>f = \theta</m>!  Technically speaking, <m>\theta = \arctan(\frac{y_0}{x_0})</m>, 
                where <m>x_0^2+y_0^2 = R^2</m>... but polar coordinates is simpler here.
              </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Why doesn't <m>f</m> define a potential function for
                    <m>\mbf F</m> in the region where <m>\mbf F</m> is defined?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-6-soln">
              <p>
                It's not a function. One input such as the point <m>(2,0)</m> has many possible outputs
                because <m>\theta</m> has infinitely many choices that correspond to that point.
              </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Hmmm...
                    Let's try to avoid the problem (the origin) by literally
                    going around the problem.
                    If <m>C</m> is the curve shown below, what is
                    <m>\work{C}{F}</m>?
                </p>

                <image width="25%" margins="0% 75%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        The closed curve in which we integrate over to find the
                        work, see description.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-7-pic3">
                        <diagram dimensions="(150,150)" margins="5">
                            <coordinates bbox="(-7,-7,7,7)">
                                <axes decorations="no"/>
                                <path start="(6,0)" stroke="blue" arrows="1" thickness="3">
                                    <arc center="(0,0)" radius="6" range="(0,345)"/>
                                    <lineto point="(2*cos(345),2*sin(345))"/>
                                </path>
                                <path start="(2*cos(345),2*sin(345))" stroke="blue" arrows="1" thickness="3">
                                    <arc center="(0,0)" radius="2" range="(345,0)"/>
                                    <lineto point="(6,0)"/>
                                </path>
                                <label anchor="(5,5)" alignment="nw" offset="(-3,3)">
                                    <m>
                                        C
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>

                    <description>
                        <p>
                            The path begins at a point on the positive
                            <m>x</m>-axis, travels right along the <m>x</m>-axis
                            for some time, then travels counterclockwise along a
                            circular arc centered at the origin until it almost
                            goes full circle, then it cuts left until just to
                            the left and below the initial starting point on the
                            positive <m>x</m>-axis, then travels clockwise along
                            a circular arc centered at the origin until it
                            connects back with the initial point.
                        </p>
                    </description>
                </image>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-6-soln">
              <p>
                <m>\work{C}{F} = \iint_D \nabla \times \mbf F ~dA = 0</m>.
              </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Now imagine the two radial pieces of <m>C</m> merging
                    together and explain why
                    <m>\ds \work{C_1}{F} = \work{C_2}{F}</m> where <m>C_1</m>
                    and <m>C_2</m> are the two circles below.
                </p>

                <image width="20%" margins="0% 80%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Two co-centric circles around the origin traveling
                        counterclockwise.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-7-pic4">
                        <diagram dimensions="(150,150)" margins="5">
                            <coordinates bbox="(-7,-7,7,7)">
                                <axes decorations="no"/>
                                <arc center="(0,0)" radius="2" range="(40,400)" arrows="1" stroke="blue" thickness="3" reverse="no" arrow-location="3"/>
                                <arc center="(0,0)" radius="6" range="(40,405)" arrows="1" stroke="blue" thickness="3" reverse="no"/>
                                <label anchor="(5,5)" alignment="nw" offset="(-3,3)">
                                    <m>
                                        C_1
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                                <label anchor="(2,2)" offset="(5,0)">
                                    <m>
                                        C_2
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>

                    <description>
                        <p>
                            Two circles are centered at the origin and oriented
                            counterclockwise, with <m>C_1</m> the circle with a
                            smaller radius and <m>C_2</m> the larger radius.
                        </p>
                    </description>
                </image>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-6-soln">
              <p>
                We have a <m>C_3</m> along the connected section on the x-axis on the right.  Then we can treat it as a closed curve similarly to
                above, where
                <md>
                  \oint_{C_1+C_3-C_2-C_3} \mbf F \cdot d\mbf r = \iint_D \nabla\times \mbf F~dA = 0.
                </md>
                (Along the connected section, <m>C_3</m> and <m>-C_3</m>, the work cancels out.). So we can simplify to
                <md>
                  \oint_{C_1-C_2} \mbf F \cdot d\mbf r = 0,
                </md>
                which implies <m>\oint_{C_1} \mbf F \cdot d\mbf r=\oint_{C_2} \mbf F \cdot d\mbf r</m>.
              </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>C_1</m> and <m>C_2</m> are the two curves shown below,
                    explain why <m>\ds \work{C_1}{F} = \work{C_2}{F}</m>.
                </p>

                <image width="25%" margins="0% 75%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Two "squiggly" co-centric curves around the origin
                        traveling counterclockwise.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-7-pic5">
                        <diagram dimensions="(150,150)" margins="5">
                            <coordinates bbox="(-7,-7,7,7)">
                                <axes decorations="no"/>
                                <spline points="((2,0),(2,1),(1,2),(-1,2),(-2,0),(-1,-2),(1,-1),(2,0))" closed="yes" stroke="blue" arrows="1" N="1000" arrow-location="2"/>
                                <spline points="((5,0),(4,4),(1,4),(-1,5),(-4,0),(-1,-5),(1,-3),(5,0))" closed="yes" stroke="blue" arrows="1" N="1000" arrow-location="1"/>
                                <label anchor="(4,3)" alignment="ne" offset="(4,3)">
                                    <m>
                                        C_1
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                                <label anchor="(3,1)" offset="(1,4)">
                                    <m>
                                        C_2
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-6-soln">
              <p>
                Same idea, draw in a connected section that you travel in both directions
                to turn this into one closed curve.  The connected section cancels 
                itself out, and we have the same argument as above.
              </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find <m>\ds \work{C}{F}</m> if <m>C</m> is the curve below.
                </p>

                <image width="25%" margins="0% 75%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        One squiggly curve traversing the origin
                        counterclockwise three times before crossing itself to
                        connect back to its initial point.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-7-pic6">
                        <diagram dimensions="(150,150)" margins="5">
                            <coordinates bbox="(-7,-7,7,7)">
                                <axes decorations="no"/>
                                <spline points="((1,1),(0.5,2),(-1,2),(-2,0),(-1,-2),(1,-2),(3,-1), (3,1),(1,4),(-2,3),(-4,0),(-2,-2),(0,-5),(3,-2),(5,0),(4,4),(1,6),(-3,5),(-6,0),(-3,-6),(1,-6),(4,-4),(6,0),(6,5),(4,6),(2.5,1),(1,1))" closed="yes" stroke="blue" arrows="1" N="1000" arrow-location="22.6"/>
                                <label anchor="(-5,4)" alignment="ne" offset="(-4,3)">
                                    <m>
                                        C
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-6-soln">
              <p>
                We know from previous work that <m>\oint_{unit \ circle} \mbf F \cdot d\mbf r = 2\pi</m>.
                We can separate out this picture as 3 different squiggly "circles", all
                having the same work as the work over the unit circle.  (The points where the 
                curve crosses itself becomes where the squiggle "circles" separate.)
                Then we add them all up, <md>
                  \oint_C \mbf F \cdot d\mbf r = 2\pi + 2\pi + 2\pi = 6\pi.
                </md>
                
              </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>